     1	# Three exceptions: triangle incidence and exceptional vertex links
     2	
     3	Alexander Richard Towell, with Claude Code (Anthropic) assistance. Proposed
     4	written lemmas with an exact check (PLAN.md steps 1 to 3). Independently
     5	reviewed by Claude (REVIEW-vertex-types.md) and by Codex (OpenAI;
     6	REVIEW-vertex-types-codex.md): sound after the fixes below. No original magnitude pattern is excluded.
     7	
     8	## Setting
     9	
    10	A pair-only open Type II shape (PLAN.md): p = Delta_u f on thirteen
    11	coordinates, c in {1,2}, p(0)=1, p(e_v)=0, ordinary quadratic coefficients
    12	m in {0,1}, quartic numerators 0/1, pair numerators -1/0 except three
    13	exceptional pairs with numerator -2 or +1. Let G be the graph of the
    14	exceptional pairs. Write the link of a pair A as the graph of quartics
    15	D containing A (edge D minus A), all of weight one here.
    16	
    17	Pair inversion 4m_A = k_A + |link A| and even link degrees (cubic
    18	integrality) give: a baseline edge (m = 1) has link C4 or C5; a +1 pair
    19	has m = 1 and link C3; a -2 pair has m = 1 (m = 0 would leave two edges)
    20	and a six-edge even simple graph: C6, two disjoint triangles (split) or
    21	two triangles sharing one vertex (bowtie).
    22	
    23	## Lemma 1 (triangle incidence)
    24	
    25	The number of tetrahedra containing a triangle {a,b,y} is the degree of y
    26	in the link of ab (all quartic weights are one), and equally of b in the
    27	link of ay. In a C3, C4, C5, C6 or two disjoint triangles it is 0 or 2. It can be 4 only at the centre of a bowtie, so only if all
    28	three of ab, ay, by are -2 pairs with bowtie links centred at the third
    29	vertex. Hence outside the shape "G a triangle, all three -2" there is no
    30	bowtie and every present triangle (one lying in at least one tetrahedron)
    31	lies in exactly two tetrahedra.
    32	
    33	## Lemma 2 (unit links at an exceptional vertex)
    34	
    35	Outside the triangle --- shape, fix a vertex x and split every -2 partner
    36	of x whose link is split into two copies, one per triangle. As in the
    37	two-exception theorems, P_x = 2d - 4 (d partners) gives a disjoint union
    38	of closed surfaces U_x with chi = 2 + s on d + s vertices, d <= 12, s the
    39	number of split partners. Degrees: 6 at a C6 partner, 3 at a +1 partner
    40	and at each split copy, 4 or 5 elsewhere; the two copies of one partner
    41	are non-adjacent with disjoint neighbourhoods. Every component has a
    42	vertex of degree at most 5, so chi >= 1 and components are spheres or
    43	projective planes. A sphere component has n = 12 - 2 n3 - n4 + n6 vertices
    44	by curvature, of which at most n3 / 2 copy pairs merge; with n6 <= 3 and
    45	at most d <= 12 merged vertices this gives n <= 12 (curvature alone would
    46	allow 15). Projective planes have at most 6 + n6 <= 9 vertices.
    47	
    48	`vertex_types.py` takes every sphere on at most 12 vertices (015
    49	enumeration) and every projective plane on at most 9 (`surfaces.brute`,
    50	a closing search with no classification theorem, cross-checked against
    51	vertex splitting from the orders 6 and 7 and the census 1, 3, 16, 134),
    52	and lists all multisets of components with a consistent assignment of
    53	the special vertices, for every type (c6, s, p) with 1 <= c6 + s + p <= 3.
    54	Result (sphere orders of a single-sphere link; "none" means no
    55	configuration at all):
    56	
    57	| c6, split, +1 | links |
    58	|---|---|
    59	| 1,0,0 | sphere, 9..11 (accepted three-type classification) |
    60	| 0,0,1 | sphere, 7 (accepted) |
    61	| 1,0,1 | sphere, 8, 9 (accepted adjacent -2,+1 root) |
    62	| 2,0,0 | sphere, 8..12 |
    63	| 0,0,2 | sphere, 5, 6, 8 |
    64	| 2,0,1 | sphere, 8..11 |
    65	| 1,0,2 | sphere, 7, 8 |
    66	| 3,0,0 | sphere, 9..12 |
    67	| 0,0,3 | none (016 star) |
    68	| 0,2,0 | two spheres, orders (5,5), (5,6), (5,8), (6,6), (6,8) |
    69	| 1,2,0 | two spheres, orders (5,7), (5,8), (6,7), (6,8) |
    70	| every other type with a split | none |
    71	
    72	So a split occurs only at a vertex with exactly two split partners, and
    73	the link of an exceptional vertex with no split partner is always a
    74	single sphere (ordinary vertices are type (0,0,0), not classified here;
    75	see CROSS-EDGES.md). In `vertex-types-result.json`, `configurations`
    76	counts combinations of component signatures, not sphere classes, and
    77	`only_single_sphere` is vacuously true on an empty row. The copy rule is
    78	checked before merging; checking disjointness after all merges is
    79	stronger and only removes candidates (Codex checked that every listed
    80	order pair keeps valid witnesses).
    81	
    82	## Corollary
    83	
    84	Outside the triangle --- shape every -2 link is C6 and every exceptional
    85	vertex link is a sphere with the degrees above. Proof: the link of a pair
    86	xb is the same graph seen from x and from b, so a split pair is split at
    87	both endpoints. The split pairs form a subgraph H of G in which, by
    88	Lemma 2, every vertex has degree exactly 2. A nonempty such H with at
    89	most three edges is a triangle of -2 pairs, which is the excluded shape.
    90	
    91	The rows 1,0,0, 0,0,1, 1,0,1 and the empty rows 0,1,0 and 0,0,3 reproduce
    92	the accepted two-exception statements and 016; they are controls, not
    93	premises.
    94	
    95	## Disconnected ordinary links (first count, PLAN.md step 4)
    96	
    97	At an ordinary w whose link is two six-vertex projective planes on A and
    98	B, every other vertex v has D_v = 6 + cross(v) and the cross edges number
    99	M = 7 + c <= 9. Using the minimum sphere order at least 6 allowed above
   100	for each exceptional vertex, the case "every ordinary vertex has cross
   101	degree at least 2" gives a cross-degree sum above 2M for 19 of the 23
   102	shapes at both constants. The four +1-heavy shapes (triangle +++, P4 +++,
   103	P3+K2 +++, 3K2 +++) meet 2M = 18 exactly at c = 2, and the universal-
   104	neighbour case needs a refinement wherever an exceptional vertex can
   105	have cross degree 0 (a +1,+1 vertex with the six-vertex link). These
   106	remain to be done; they are not claimed here. The triangle --- row does
   107	not use Lemma 2 (which excludes that shape): P_x = 2d - 4 with pair
   108	inversion gives d >= 6 + n(-2) - n(+1)/2 directly, so each of its
   109	vertices has d >= 8 and the bound is 24 > 18. When the cross-degree 0
   110	and 1 cases are done, the 006 six-vertex-link obstruction must be
   111	re-checked with up to six exceptional vertices present.
   112	
   113	## Scope
   114	
   115	Lemma 2 assumes no bowtie, so the triangle --- shape and the doubled-
   116	quartic shape 1 need separate treatment. Conditional on the 015 sphere
   117	enumeration (Steinitz-Rademacher, pynauty) and the identities above.
